page RecipeSignInWithASessionCookie(path: "/recipes/sign-in-with-a-session-cookie", title: "Sign in with a session cookie — Recipes", description: "Sign a reader in with an httpOnly session cookie: an api with credentials: .sameOrigin, a Session store, and a guarded account page.") {
    RecipeShell("Sign in with a session cookie", summary: "A token kept in the page can be read by any script that reaches it. To sign a reader in without holding a token at all, let the server set a session cookie, describe the service with `credentials: .sameOrigin`, and keep the signed-in user in a store:", outputs: ["website", "app"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Page through results", to: "/recipes/page-through-results"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Load more as the reader asks", to: "/recipes/load-more"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Update at once, and roll back on failure", to: "/recipes/optimistic-updates")], source: "md-docs/recipes/sign-in-with-a-session-cookie.md", how: true, hasVariations: false, project: {}) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** a server that answers the `/api` endpoints the `api` block names and sets the session cookie. The code declares `type User` and an `app`, as the starter does: paste it into a new project, replacing `src/App.wf` and the starter's `src/stores/auth.wf`, and deleting `src/pages/Profile.wf`, which reads the starter's `AuthStore`.")
            DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type Credentials { email: String, password: String }

/// Why signing in can be refused.
enum SignInError {
    unauthorized(problem: Problem) on 401
    http(error: HttpError)
}

api Auth(base: "/api") {
    credentials: .sameOrigin
    get me at "me" -> User!
    post signIn(body: Credentials) at "signIn" -> User ! SignInError
    post signOut at "signOut" -> !
}

store Session {
    state user: User? = null
    state checked = false
    derived signedIn = user != null
    action load() {
        match await Auth.me() {
            .ok(u) { user = u }
            .err(_) { user = null }
        }
        checked = true
    }
    action signIn(email: String, password: String) -> ! SignInError {
        user = try await Auth.signIn(body: Credentials(email: email, password: password))
        navigate("/account")
    }
    action signOut() -> ! {
        try await Auth.signOut()
        user = null
        Auth.me.invalidate()
        navigate("/")
    }
}

page SignIn(path: "/sign-in", title: "Sign in", description: "Sign in to your account.", noindex: true) {
    state email = ""
    state password = ""
    state problem = ""
    validate email { required  email }
    validate password { required }
    action send() {
        problem = ""
        match await Session.signIn(email, password) {
            .ok { }
            .err(.unauthorized(_)) { problem = "That email and password do not match." }
            .err(.http(e)) { problem = e.message }
        }
    }
    Heading("Sign in").h1
    if problem != "" { Alert(problem).danger }
    Form(bind: form) {
        on submit { send() }
        Input(bind: email, label: "Email").email
        Input(bind: password, label: "Password").password
        Button("Sign in", type: .submit, disabled: send.pending).primary
    }
}

page Account(path: "/account", title: "Your account", description: "Your account.", guard: !Session.checked || Session.signedIn, redirect: "/sign-in") {
    use Session
    // The guard is read when the route opens, which on a reload is before
    // `load` has answered; this sends the reader on once it has.
    effect { if Session.checked && !Session.signedIn { navigate("/sign-in") } }
    Heading("Your account").h1
    if !Session.checked { Spinner }
    if let u = Session.user {
        Text("Signed in as {u.name}")
        Button("Sign out") { on click { Session.signOut() } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")
            Markdown("The app asks the server who is signed in once, when the site opens, in `src/App.wf`:")
            DocsCodeBlock(#"app {
    use Session
    Session.load()
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("- `Auth` [describes the service](/learn/15-data#describe-a-service-with-api). `credentials: .sameOrigin` sends the session cookie with every call, so the page holds no token. `SignInError` names what a refused sign-in answers, `on 401`, and keeps `http` for every other failure.\n- The `Session` [store](/learn/11-stores#a-first-store) holds the user, or `null`, and `signedIn` is derived from it. `load` asks the server who is signed in, `signIn` and `signOut` call the service and move the reader on with `navigate`.\n- The server sets the cookie when a sign-in succeeds, with a header such as `Set-Cookie: session=…; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=1209600`. `HttpOnly` keeps it from every script, `SameSite=Lax` from another site's form, and `Secure` off plaintext connections.\n- `Auth.me.invalidate()` on sign-out drops what the endpoint cached, and any `resource` over it asks again, so the next reader of the tab is not shown the last one's. Do the same for every endpoint whose answer is one reader's: an answer cached under one `Authorization` header is never served for another, but a cookie cannot be read, so the cache cannot tell one cookie's reader from the next.\n- The `SignIn` page validates its two fields and matches the result of `Session.signIn`, so a wrong password shows an `Alert` instead of throwing. `send.pending` disables the button while the request runs, and `noindex: true` keeps the page out of search results.\n- `Session.load()` at the top of `app` runs once, when the site opens: a reader who comes back with a session cookie is signed in again, and `checked` turns true when the server has answered either way.\n- The `Account` page has a [guard](/learn/05-pages-and-routing#guards). A guard is read once, when the route opens, so it lets the page through while `checked` is still false, and the `effect` sends a signed-out reader to `/sign-in` as soon as the answer arrives. Until then the page shows a `Spinner`.\n\nThe session is an httpOnly cookie the server sets, so the page never holds a token. `guard:` hides the page from a reader who is signed out, and the server still checks every request ([Security](/about/security#where-a-session-belongs)).")

    }
}
